{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786428899,"nonce":"rpFHSz0XugK9iIv59QCSULFOmbc=","message":"Fixed. grouping/service gained JoinMany: the batch joins every member, then recomputes the rollup ONCE and evaluates storm ONCE. app/adapters.go joinMembers collects and makes one call. Join survives as a delegation.\n\nThe waste was worse than filed: Join ended with recompute AND evaluateStorm AND publish, so 500 members cost 500 Rollup aggregates, 500 SetRollup CAS writes, 500 DistinctJoinsSince window queries and 500 ui_events frames describing the same generation — plus 500 orgs.settings reads outside the transaction. Now one of each, in one InTx instead of 500.\n\nStorm semantics preserved: evaluateStorm never counted its own invocations (it queries DistinctJoinsSince over alert_group_members), ApplyStorm already returned changed=false when already in the target mode, so it was always one notice per batch. One observable change, a strict improvement: storm mode now flips at the END of the batch, so the event reads distinct_alerts=500 rather than 26.\n\nTest with counting fakes asserts Rollup==1 and SetRollup==1 while members.Join==500 and member_joined events==500 — batching the projection must not batch away the membership or its timeline.\n\nNote: the issue's line numbers were wrong (joinMembers is at 1019-1048, not 909-919), and partitionByGroup already guarantees one group per call, so the suggested 'collect the distinct group ids' step was redundant.","files":null}]}